From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bartlomiej Dudzik <15948633+BobrImperator@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 19:11:55 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] renetcode: harden server handshake and token admission (D2,
 D3, D7-D9)

D2. Unauthenticated or rejected packets refreshed server liveness. Liveness
is now written only for an accepted Payload or KeepAlive, a valid request
retransmission, and a Response that passed every promotion check.

D3. A challenge issued for one connect token could be replayed under another
pending connection's keys. A Response whose challenge client id or user data
differs from the pending connection's is rejected, and the duplicate-identity
check uses the pending connection's client id.

D7. The global sequence and each connection's sequence both started at 0
under the same server-to-client key, so the Challenge and the first KeepAlive
reused a ChaCha20-Poly1305 nonce. Global packets now use the upper half of
the sequence space and per-connection packets the lower half. Every encode
site takes its sequence from `allocate_sequence`, which refuses at the range
end and never wraps.

D8. A connect token could be admitted again after its session ended. Each
history entry now carries the token MAC, source address, authenticated
expiry and Pending/Consumed state. A token is admitted only while Pending, at
its original address and unexpired; it is consumed before the first
per-connection packet is encrypted. New tokens take only empty or expired
slots and are refused while the history is full.

D9. A restarted Secure server accepted tokens consumed before the restart.
`ServerAuthentication::Secure` gains `max_connect_token_lifetime`, and the
server refuses any token that expires before its startup time plus that
lifetime. This is an API break; wire formats are unchanged. The echo example
passes the lifetime it issues tokens with.

The fixes share the pending-connection and promotion code, so they are one
change. Includes the server regression and adversarial corpus tests.
